---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import AfterTaxCalc from '../../components/calc/AfterTaxCalc';
import FundPair from '../../components/FundPair.astro';
import YieldChart from '../../components/YieldChart.astro';
import { fund, pct, usd, day, drag, HISTORY } from '../../lib/data';
import { afterTax } from '../../lib/engine/tax';
import { src, related, METHOD, PAGE_FUNDS } from '../../lib/common';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';
const a = fund('SPAXX'), b = fund('VMFXX');
// Same example as the calculator defaults below and the side-by-side table (test naïf 2026-11-08: the text said joint New York, the calculator showed single).
const s = { amount: 50000, state: 'CA', filing: 'single' as const, income: 120000 };
const nyA = afterTax(a, s), nyB = afterTax(b, s);
const title = fitTitle(['SPAXX vs VMFXX: Fidelity and Vanguard Cash After Tax', 'SPAXX vs VMFXX: Fidelity or Vanguard Cash Fund After Tax']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `SPAXX vs VMFXX as filed: ${pct(a.y)} against ${pct(b.y)}. The default cash funds of Fidelity and Vanguard compared on cost, holdings and yield after your state tax.`,
  `SPAXX vs VMFXX as filed: ${pct(a.y)} against ${pct(b.y)}. The default cash funds of Fidelity and Vanguard compared on cost, holdings and yield after state tax.`,
]);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is VMFXX better than SPAXX?', a: `On the filed figures VMFXX yielded ${pct(b.y)} and SPAXX ${pct(a.y)}, mostly because SPAXX keeps more for expenses: about ${pct(drag(a) ?? 0)} of its gross yield against ${pct(drag(b) ?? 0)}. Better for you depends on where your account is and what moving it costs, which this site does not judge.` },
  { q: 'Which has more state-exempt income, SPAXX or VMFXX?', a: `At the last month end VMFXX held about ${pct(b.usob, 0)} in Treasuries and exempt agency debt and SPAXX about ${pct(a.usob, 0)}. Both passed the 50% quarter-end test in June and September. The year-end letters of each company give the share of dividends to use on a return.` },
  { q: 'Can I hold VMFXX at Fidelity?', a: `Some brokerages let customers buy other companies’ money market funds, sometimes with a transaction fee or a different share class, and some do not. The Form N-MFP filing says nothing about where a fund can be bought; check the brokerage’s fund list and fee schedule.` },
];
---
<ToolPage id="spaxx-vs-vmfxx" title={title} description={description} crumb="SPAXX vs VMFXX"
  h1="SPAXX vs VMFXX: the two big cash funds after tax"
  lede={`SPAXX and VMFXX are where Fidelity and Vanguard put the uninvested cash of a brokerage account. Here is what each leaves you after tax, from the yields they reported for ${day(a.yDate)} (SPAXX) and ${day(b.yDate)} (VMFXX). Not investment advice.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={related('fund-spaxx', 'fund-vmfxx', 'money-market-vs-high-yield-savings')} sources={src('nmfp', 'ny-it201i', 'irs-rp', 'irs-niit')}>
  <AfterTaxCalc client:load funds={PAGE_FUNDS} a="SPAXX" b="VMFXX" pick={false} methodHref={METHOD} id="c-sv" />
  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-10 max-w-none">
    <h2>Two default cash funds, one gap that does not close</h2>
    <p>Most people who own SPAXX or VMFXX did not choose them: they are where cash lands in a Fidelity or a Vanguard brokerage account. That makes the comparison a question about the account, and the filings answer the money part of it. VMFXX reported a 7-day net yield of {pct(b.y)} and SPAXX {pct(a.y)}. Their gross yields, what the portfolios earned before expenses, were much closer: {pct(b.g ?? 0)} and {pct(a.g ?? 0)}. Most of the gap is therefore cost, not strategy. The portfolios do differ in shape. SPAXX holds more agency repo and longer floating-rate agency notes, which pushed its weighted average life, the average time until holdings are paid back in full, to {a.wal} days against {b.wal} for VMFXX; VMFXX held more Federal Home Loan Bank and Farm Credit debt. Neither difference changes the tax picture much: both funds earned most of their income from obligations that states may not tax, and both cleared the 50% quarter-end test.</p>
    <p>The calculator above starts from a single filer in California with $120,000 of taxable income and $50,000 in cash. With those numbers VMFXX leaves {pct(nyB.afterTaxYield)} and SPAXX {pct(nyA.afterTaxYield)}, a difference of {usd(Math.round(nyB.net) - Math.round(nyA.net))} a year. Whether that justifies moving an account is a judgment about everything else the account does.</p>
  </div>
  <YieldChart series={[{ label: 'SPAXX', points: HISTORY.SPAXX, color: '#1F5C4A' }, { label: 'VMFXX', points: HISTORY.VMFXX, color: '#B45309' }]} caption="Daily 7-day net yields as filed." />
  <FundPair tickers={['SPAXX', 'VMFXX']} heading="SPAXX and VMFXX side by side" />
</ToolPage>
